  "Question": "Let \\(f(t)=\\dfrac{t}{1-t},\\) \\(t\\ne1.\\) If \\(y=f(x),\\) then \\(x\\) can be expressed as:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(f\\left(\\dfrac1y\\right)\\)",
    "B": "\\(-f(y)\\)",
    "C": "\\(-f(-y)\\)",
    "D": "\\(f(-y)\\)",
    "E": "\\(f(y)\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "To recover the input \\(x\\), start with the given relation and isolate \\(x\\). Since \\(x\\ne1\\), multiplying by \\(1-x\\) is valid:\n\\[\ny=\\frac{x}{1-x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny(1-x)=x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny=x(1+y).\n\\]\nBefore dividing by \\(1+y\\), check that it cannot be zero. The original relation gives\n\\[\n1+y=1+\\frac{x}{1-x}=\\frac1{1-x}\\ne0.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nx=\\frac{y}{1+y}.\n\\]\nUsing the definition of \\(f\\), we also have\n\\[\n-f(-y)=-\\frac{-y}{1-(-y)}=\\frac{y}{1+y}.\n\\]\nThis function value is defined because \\(y\\ne-1\\). Therefore \\(x=-f(-y)\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
